- (application code + depenedencies + runtime + configuration) => into an isolated environment => called container.
- advantages of container
   - really easy to spin up containers.
    - easy to replace containers.
     - solve common issue - "work on my machine but not working on server"
    - scallable - create as many copies as you want.
- problems with containers
  - host machine runs out of resources.
  - containers die and get ignored ( no one get notified), k8s handles this using kube-controller of control plane.
  - single point of failure (Host machine goes down, taking everything with it)

- k8s containers are placed inside kubernetes object called POD.
- PODs are the wrappers placed around the containers.
- k8s manages POD, not containers.
- POD need to live on top of some environment so that underlying container get the required resources, such host can be physical/virtual machine, called NODE.
- k8s can be multiple NODEs, significantly increased the resources required by the lots of PODs. When we conbine all of such nodes together, we get k8s CLUSTER.


